WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.339 --> 00:00:06.779
Did you know that your brain sees more

00:00:06.779 --> 00:00:10.200
than you do? Let me explain. In order for

00:00:10.200 --> 00:00:12.330
us to understand and interact with the

00:00:12.330 --> 00:00:14.580
world around us, our brain must process,

00:00:14.580 --> 00:00:17.090
or make sense of, what the eye see.

00:00:17.090 --> 00:00:19.470
Information travels from the eyes

00:00:19.470 --> 00:00:21.240
through the brain getting processed

00:00:21.240 --> 00:00:24.510
along the way. The first, most basic stage

00:00:24.510 --> 00:00:27.029
of processing occurs at a region called

00:00:27.029 --> 00:00:30.750
the primary visual cortex. Afterward, the

00:00:30.750 --> 00:00:32.880
processing becomes increasingly complex.

00:00:32.880 --> 00:00:35.460
It divides into two streams with

00:00:35.460 --> 00:00:38.040
distinct functions: the ventral stream

00:00:38.040 --> 00:00:39.899
flows along the lower region of the

00:00:39.899 --> 00:00:42.390
brain. It is often called the what

00:00:42.390 --> 00:00:44.700
pathway because processing in these

00:00:44.700 --> 00:00:48.170
regions allows to identify what we see.

00:00:48.170 --> 00:00:51.000
The dorsal stream flows along the top

00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:53.219
region of the brain. It is called the

00:00:53.219 --> 00:00:56.250
where or how pathway because it helps us

00:00:56.250 --> 00:00:58.920
to identify where objects are and how to

00:00:58.920 --> 00:01:01.620
interact with them. The dorsal stream

00:01:01.620 --> 00:01:03.960
allows us to use vision to make precise

00:01:03.960 --> 00:01:07.470
effective movements. One of the ways we

00:01:07.470 --> 00:01:09.540
know about these two separate streams is

00:01:09.540 --> 00:01:11.760
because of evidence from patients with

00:01:11.760 --> 00:01:14.460
very specific brain damage. Sometimes

00:01:14.460 --> 00:01:16.799
people can sustain damage to one visual

00:01:16.799 --> 00:01:20.520
stream but not the other. One famous

00:01:20.520 --> 00:01:23.850
patient known as DF lost function of her

00:01:23.850 --> 00:01:26.549
what pathway in the ventral stream but

00:01:26.549 --> 00:01:28.740
was still able to interact with objects

00:01:28.740 --> 00:01:31.020
using the how pathway in the dorsal

00:01:31.020 --> 00:01:33.780
stream. For example, she was not able to

00:01:33.780 --> 00:01:36.479
describe the orientation of a slot she

00:01:36.479 --> 00:01:39.390
was clearly able to see but was able to

00:01:39.390 --> 00:01:41.670
reach out and insert a card into the

00:01:41.670 --> 00:01:43.380
slot so even though she couldn't

00:01:43.380 --> 00:01:46.140
perceive the orientation her brain could

00:01:46.140 --> 00:01:50.310
use the information to guide action. This

00:01:50.310 --> 00:01:53.250
condition is known as visual agnosia. The

00:01:53.250 --> 00:01:55.890
reverse is called optic ataxia and it's

00:01:55.890 --> 00:01:59.280
more rare but can also occur. Damage to

00:01:59.280 --> 00:02:01.200
the how pathway in the dorsal stream

00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:03.600
but not the what pathway in the ventral

00:02:03.600 --> 00:02:06.000
stream means that optic ataxia patients

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:09.149
can't use objects correctly but can still

00:02:09.149 --> 00:02:11.170
perceive what they are.

00:02:11.170 --> 00:02:13.490
Researchers have also shown the same

00:02:13.490 --> 00:02:15.709
separation between perception and action

00:02:15.709 --> 00:02:18.440
in healthy participants by using visual

00:02:18.440 --> 00:02:21.170
illusions that make objects seem larger

00:02:21.170 --> 00:02:23.420
or smaller than they actually are. Take a

00:02:23.420 --> 00:02:25.550
look at this image. When we look at the

00:02:25.550 --> 00:02:28.190
circles the illusion tricks our ventral

00:02:28.190 --> 00:02:30.069
stream into perceiving this one as

00:02:30.069 --> 00:02:32.870
larger than this one even though they're

00:02:32.870 --> 00:02:35.480
actually the same size. But when we reach

00:02:35.480 --> 00:02:38.000
out to pick up a circle even if we

00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:40.459
perceive it as larger than it is we are

00:02:40.459 --> 00:02:42.650
able to grasp it based on its actual

00:02:42.650 --> 00:02:45.250
size thanks to the dorsal stream.

00:02:45.250 --> 00:02:47.690
Essentially, the illusion fools the

00:02:47.690 --> 00:02:50.080
ventral stream but not the dorsal stream.

00:02:50.080 --> 00:02:52.819
This separation between perception and

00:02:52.819 --> 00:02:54.950
action in patients and healthy

00:02:54.950 --> 00:02:57.350
participants shows that the two visual

00:02:57.350 --> 00:02:59.660
processing streams and their functions

00:02:59.660 --> 00:03:02.299
are independent. That being said in

00:03:02.299 --> 00:03:04.640
everyday life it's rare you'd ever

00:03:04.640 --> 00:03:06.410
notice the difference between vision for

00:03:06.410 --> 00:03:08.989
perception and vision for action. The

00:03:08.989 --> 00:03:10.850
dorsal and ventral streams work together

00:03:10.850 --> 00:03:13.269
to help us perform daily tasks and

00:03:13.269 --> 00:03:15.440
researchers are continuing to study the

00:03:15.440 --> 00:03:18.290
exact relationship between the two. So

00:03:18.290 --> 00:03:19.609
even though your brain sees more than

00:03:19.609 --> 00:03:22.340
you do it's very good at integrating all

00:03:22.340 --> 00:03:24.079
of your vision into one seamless

00:03:24.079 --> 00:03:26.570
experience of the world and allowing you

00:03:26.570 --> 00:04:02.669
to interact with it!

